of Mill Pond Rapid","poi_name":"Mill Pond Rapid","subject":"","author":"Trent Ganstine","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/662044","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/662044"}},{"id":"17879","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2670","description":"","caption":"first solo trip","poi_name":"","subject":"Daelan Butler","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/17879","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/17879"}},{"id":"42551","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"10167","description":"","caption":"Ben Surfing","poi_name":"","subject":"Ben Burns","author":"Dan Case","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42551","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42551"}},{"id":"42550","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"10167","description":"","caption":"suction","poi_name":"","subject":"Dan Case","author":"Dan Case","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42550","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42550"}},{"id":"42552","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"10167","description":"","caption":"Boof","poi_name":"","subject":"Kevin Miller","author":"Dan Case","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42552","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42552"}},{"id":"881151","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"883654","description":"Low-ish water","caption":"Branham Mill","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/881151","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/881151"}}],"detail":{"id":"1722","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"2008-07-07 01:28:11\n\nThis was a great river but would have much better with about 4 more inches of water. Ran this on 4/25/2008 and it wasy very low. This would be a good run after a good 2 or 3 day rain. After long periods of dry weather, Bledsoe Creek is very \"flashy,\" it rises and falls quickly after heavy rains. In dry conditions you can run it in the first couple of hours after a heavy thunderstorm - 3/4 of an inch in a short time. During wet periods you can run it within 24 hours of a 3/4 to 1 inch rainfall. Check rainfall totals online and look for Gallatin, Bethpage, and Lafayette rainfall totals. If they average at least 3/4 of an inch, it is usually good to go.\n\n2004-07-16 11:23:28\n\nAllen Brown\n\nAt med. - high water the bottom hole is just as good for play with a hugh eddy on river left to recover and go again. The bottom hole is so deep it is hard to see the top of the surfer's head from the eddy. Edit\n\n2003-02-28 15:38:55\n\nClay Wright\n\nBledsoe is mostly class 1, 1-2 with one 'major' drop at an old Mill site (class 2). The land on both sides is private property and they are wary of lawsuits but the top hole looks playable at med-high flows.\n\nScouted only -\n\nClay","averageGradient":"0","maxGradient":"0","length":"7","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111440","116710"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-86.32532910977943,36.455618810080296],[-86.3255035097792,36.455626276746955],[-86.3257169764455,36.45552447674709],[-86.3259367097785,36.455244410080866],[-86.32605177644501,36.45486981008145],[-86.32617910977814,36.45452941008199],[-86.32665830977737,36.4536954100833],[-86.32687870977708,36.45331187675055],[-86.32700430977684,36.452984476751055],[-86.3270717764434,36.45246047675187],[-86.32721857644322,36.45196927675261],[-86.32732417644303,36.45173301008634],[-86.32769157644248,36.45076001008783],[-86.32804010977526,36.4499344100891],[-86.32818357644